{
  // Cloudflare Workers config.
  //
  // NO SECRETS, NO ACCOUNT ID — those live in CI and in `wrangler secret`
  // (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §12). Nothing here has been provisioned: no Worker,
  // no R2 bucket, no Turnstile key, no DNS change. This file only declares the
  // shape of the eventual deployment.
  //
  // `main` and the assets binding are supplied by @astrojs/cloudflare at build
  // time — do not hand-set them here, or the Cloudflare Vite plugin will try to
  // resolve a file that does not exist yet.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "dev",

  // PINNED TO THE INSTALLED workerd, not to "today".
  //
  // wrangler 4.128.0 bundles workerd 1.20260831.1, whose newest supported compatibility
  // date is 2026-08-31 (`@astrojs/cloudflare/info` reports it). The previous value here
  // was 2026-09-01 — one day AHEAD of it — which means `wrangler dev` and
  // `npm run preview:cloudflare` silently run under DIFFERENT semantics than the deployed
  // Worker, because the edge honours the date and the local runtime cannot. A local
  // preview that is not the same runtime as production is a preview that can only ever
  // agree with production by luck.
  //
  // Raise this together with wrangler, never on its own.
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-31",

  // Kept, and it is the documented @astrojs/cloudflare configuration. The current bundle
  // imports no `node:` specifier at all, so nothing WOULD fail today — but the flag also
  // governs the `process` / `Buffer` shims the Astro runtime reaches for, and dropping it
  // to save nothing measurable is how a deploy breaks on a route nobody built yet.
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

  // Worker logs, on. This is the only visibility a dev deployment has: there is no
  // error tracker, and `npm run validate:*` cannot see a runtime 500.
  "observability": { "enabled": true },

  // ── STEP 14 · TWO DEPLOYMENT TARGETS, AND THEY CAN NEVER OVERWRITE EACH OTHER ──────
  //
  // Wrangler environments, with an EXPLICIT `name` in each one. Explicit, because the
  // default is to append the environment name to the top-level name — which would have
  // renamed the existing staging Worker out from under every measurement in
  // docs/STAGING_DEPLOY.md §8. Step 13's QA describes a specific artefact at a specific
  // URL; repurposing that URL would silently invalidate the record rather than update it.
  //
  //   dev   Worker `dev`
  //         https://dev.nextvisionfilms.com  (also https://dev.next-vision-films.workers.dev)
  //         Deployed by CI on every push to the `dev` branch.
  //
  //   prod  Worker `next-vision-films-production`
  //         https://www.nextvisionfilms.com  (and nextvisionfilms.com)
  //         Deployed by CI only on a push to `master`. THE LIVE SITE.
  //
  // RENAMED 2026-10-22 from `staging` / `production`, at the user's request. The dev
  // Worker was renamed too (it was `next-vision-films`, now deleted). The prod Worker
  // deliberately KEEPS its name: nextvisionfilms.com and www are Custom Domains bound to
  // THAT Worker, and a deploy under a new name would create a second Worker without
  // them — the domain would keep serving the old build while the new one went nowhere,
  // and deleting the old Worker would take the site offline. Never rename it.
  //
  // CUSTOM DOMAINS ARE ATTACHED IN CLOUDFLARE, NOT HERE. `www`, the apex and `dev` are
  // Workers Custom Domains bound to their Worker in the dashboard/API, and wrangler leaves
  // a Worker's custom domains alone when this file declares no `routes` — which is why they
  // survive every CI deploy. Declaring them here instead would make every deploy re-assert
  // them, and the CI token would then need zone DNS permissions it does not have.
  //
  // The top-level `name` is the dev name ON PURPOSE: a bare `wrangler deploy`, with no
  // --env, is then a mistake that lands on dev. Reaching prod requires typing `--env
  // prod`. The dangerous direction is the one that needs the flag.
  //
  // Nothing else differs between the two. Every deferred capability — the media
  // hostname, the analytics token, the Turnstile keys, the contact recipient — is unset
  // in BOTH, because an unset value already has a defined, safe meaning everywhere
  // (CLAUDE.md rule 19). Neither environment may acquire a capability the other has not
  // been granted without that being a deliberate, recorded decision.
  //
  // `compatibility_date`, `compatibility_flags` and `observability` are inheritable keys
  // and are NOT repeated below: repeating them is how the two runtimes drift apart.
  "env": {
    "dev": {
      "name": "dev",
      // The ONE deliberate difference (docs/ADMIN_DASHBOARD_PLAN.md §3.1, decision A3): the
      // owners' admin exists only on dev, behind Cloudflare Access. These identify the Access
      // team and application the Worker verifies tokens against. Neither is a secret. Unset,
      // the admin refuses every request; prod has no admin route at all.
      "vars": {
        "ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN": "royal-hall-6f88.cloudflareaccess.com",
        "ACCESS_AUD": "f1b7ab18b88a7e1085ce1e271598a9d1b31d750c195b39552a3b44d8a6abe722"
      }
    },
    "prod": {
      "name": "next-vision-films-production"
    }
  }

  // NO `vars` BLOCK. It used to declare
  //     "PUBLIC_MEDIA_BASE_URL": "https://media.nextvisionfilms.com"
  // for a host that has never existed, and Vite substituted it into every built page —
  // so a local build emitted links to nothing while ALSO copying the local media mirror
  // into dist. The variable now has exactly one source: the environment (see .env.example
  // and docs/MEDIA_PIPELINE.md §4). Unset, the site serves its own media and works with no
  // network at all; set, it serves from R2. Add it back here only once the delivery
  // hostname is real.
  //
  // Bindings deliberately absent until the resources actually exist:
  //   r2_buckets                       — the site reads media over plain HTTPS, so it needs
  //                                      no binding at all; uploads use the S3 API from a
  //                                      developer machine (scripts/media-sync.mjs)
  //   kv_namespaces (SESSION)          — only if sessions are ever needed; they are not
  //   send_email                       — Cloudflare Email Routing's own sending binding.
  //                                      The lowest-infrastructure delivery option there
  //                                      is, and unavailable until the zone is ON
  //                                      Cloudflare with Email Routing enabled (D20).
  //                                      docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §7.
  //
  // ── PHASE 13 · the contact endpoint's configuration ────────────────────────
  //
  // `src/pages/api/contact.ts` is the one route with `prerender = false`, so this Worker
  // now does something. It reads its configuration from the ENVIRONMENT at request time
  // via `cloudflare:workers` — NOT from `Astro.locals.runtime.env`, which @astrojs/
  // cloudflare 14 removed and which throws on access.
  //
  // SECRETS — `wrangler secret put NAME`, never a `vars` entry, never this file:
  //     TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY     required. Unset, every submission is refused with 503.
  //     RESEND_API_KEY           whichever provider is chosen (decision D17)
  //     BREVO_API_KEY
  //
  // NON-SECRET, but still not here while D17/D2 are open — a recipient in version control
  // is a recipient nobody reviewed. Set them with `wrangler secret put` too, or add a
  // `vars` block the day the values are decided:
  //     CONTACT_PROVIDER         "resend" | "brevo"
  //     CONTACT_RECIPIENT        where an accepted message goes            <- D17 / D2
  //     CONTACT_SENDER           From:, on a domain the provider verified
  //
  // BUILD-TIME, not a Worker variable — it is inlined into the HTML by Vite:
  //     PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY
  //
  // Local overrides for all of the above go in `.dev.vars` (gitignored). See
  // .env.example and scripts/test-forms.mjs.
  //
  // NOT added, and deliberately: no KV and no D1. Turnstile plus the honeypot plus
  // server-side validation is the abuse control, and edge rate limiting on /api/* is a
  // WAF rule in the dashboard rather than infrastructure this project has to own.
}
